    ex:sectionMain "3.2 DEFORESTATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.2.4 As a change in land cover and use" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.1 AFFORESTATION - Afforestation may be a legal term or a process of stocking land. In the case of the latter, we group the definitions into those that imply a change in land cover only and those that imply a change in land cover and use. Four definitions specify \"crops\" implying that the trees will be later removed for commercial purposes. The time period for lands \"never having been forested\" vary from never to 30 to 100 years." ;
    skos:note "(clearing) (Rodung) - A land use change of forest land to other uses. Deforestation (clearing) is forbidden in Austria (due to former laws deforestation (clearing) is countrywide forbidden in Austria since about 150 years). However, if public interest in the different land use is higher than public interest in the conservation of the forest, the authority may give its approval for the deforestation (clearing). Usually, as a compensation other adequate areas have to be afforested. (Austrian Forestry Act (Federal Legal Gazette no. 440/1975, as amended Federal Legal Gazette 231/1977, 142/1978 and 576/1987) From: Weiss Peter weissp@ubavie.gv.at)"@en .

ex:Def_def_aus_1975_1650 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(Wiederbewaldung): Reforestation areas are temporarily unstocked areas caused by..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Austria ;
    ex:country "Austria" ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.4 FORESTATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.5.1 Restoration of Land Cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "(Wiederbewaldung): Reforestation areas are temporarily unstocked areas caused by harvesting, wind breaks, natural disasters and so on. These areas have to be reforested artificially (usually within 3 years, under certain circumstances within maximal 8 years) or with methods of natural regeneration (usually within 8 years, under certain circumstances within maximal 11 years). In Austria reforestation has always been recognized as a part of forest management and has never been linked up to land use change. (Austrian Forestry Act (Federal Legal Gazette no. 440/1975, as amended Federal Legal Gazette 231/1977, 142/1978 and 576/1987) From: Weiss Peter weissp@ubavie.gv.at)"@en .

ex:Def_def_aus_1977_815 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "An area stocked by trees (a list of tree species in this context is given in an ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Austria ;
    ex:country "Austria" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_aus_1977_815_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "An area stocked by trees (a list of tree species in this context is given in an annex to the Austrian Forestry Act), if the stocking of trees represents an area of at least 1000 m2 and is of an average width of at least 10 m. Forest areas, which are unstocked due to forest management practices (for instance harvesting areas or areas used for timber storage, skidding tracks or forest roads), are still referred to as \"forest land.\" (Austrian Forestry Act (Federal Legal Gazette no. 440/1975, as amended Federal Legal Gazette 231/1977, 142/1978 and 576/1987) From: Weiss Peter weissp@ubavie.gv.at)"@en .

