ex:Def_def_den_1992_848 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Areas covered with species capable of producing wood that grow to a height of mo..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Denmark ;
    ex:country "Denmark" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_den_1992_848_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_United_Nations ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Areas covered with species capable of producing wood that grow to a height of more than 6 m. Forest or plantation shall be more than 0.5 ha in size. Forest area should be wider than 20 m (no hedgerows) although narrow sections are allowed as part of a larger forest. The forest area has -- or is expected to have within a short period -- a crown density of at least 50 percent. Older stands of Christmas trees on arable land are considered forest, if at the time of inventory, they were in such a condition the wood production was at least the same economic importance as the production of Christmas trees. (UN/ECE 1992 p. 140)"@en .

ex:Def_def_den_1992_848_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "50.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "50.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "6.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "6.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_den_1998_1661 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Reforestation forests with a forest-obligation written in the cadastre must be r..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Denmark ;
    ex:country "Denmark" ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.4 FORESTATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.5.1 Restoration of Land Cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "Reforestation forests with a forest-obligation written in the cadastre must be reforested, if not deforestation takes place and that is forbidden without permission from the national forest-authorities. A forest obligation states that the area in principle shall be used for forests purposes forever (so-called 'good and sustainable forestry'). If the owner can give the forest authorities a very good reason - for instance the soil is not suited for forestry - he can have permission to raise the cadastre-note, but normally he shall find another agriculture land of a bigger size to plant with forest, for instance a double size. (From: PMP@sns.dk Date: Tue, 29 Sep 1998 09:31:59 +0100)"@en .

ex:Def_def_den_1998_845 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "More than 0.5 ha, broader than 30 m, covered by tree-species which are or is abl..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Denmark ;
    ex:country "Denmark" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_den_1998_845_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "More than 0.5 ha, broader than 30 m, covered by tree-species which are or is able to be more that 6 m's in height. (From: PMP@sns.dk Date: Tue, 29 Sep 1998 09:31:59 +0100). Note: at first glance this appears to be a land cover description, but subsequent definitions of afforestation and reforestation for Denmark show that \"forest\" is really a land use term"@en .

ex:Def_def_den_1998_845_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "30.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "30.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_den_2004_436 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "A forest is a tree-grown area which is larger than ½ hectare and is more than 20..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Denmark ;
    ex:country "Denmark" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_den_2004_436_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "A forest is a tree-grown area which is larger than ½ hectare and is more than 20 metres wide. The trees shall be able to grow to a highstemmed closed forest inside a reasonable timespan, cf. the Danish Nature Agency's guidelines of 1 October 2004 on the Forest Act"@en .

ex:Def_def_den_2004_436_criteria ex:minHeight "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidth "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidthSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_den_2006_846 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Use Min. Area (ha) = 0.5 Min. Crown Cover (%) = 10 Min. Tree Height (m) = 5 Min...." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Denmark ;
    ex:country "Denmark" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_den_2006_846_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UNFCCC ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Use Min. Area (ha) = 0.5 Min. Crown Cover (%) = 10 Min. Tree Height (m) = 5 Min. Strip Width (m) = 20 (2006) In addition, the forest area includes temporarily unstocked areas, smaller open areas in the forest needed for management purposes and fire breaks. Forests in national parks, reserves, or areas under special protection are included. Windbreaks and groves covering more than 0.5 ha and with a minimum width of 20 m are also considered as forests. Farmlands, orchards, gardens (houses and summer houses) are NOT included in the forest area"@en .

