    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_est_unknown_854_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Forest land - Land which fulfils at least one of the following: 1) Trees are >1.3 m high, crown density is >30%; 2) Land is or it is intended to be managed to get timber and other forest products or where trees are conserved for use in other ways (nature conservation, environment protection, sanitary protection, recreation, hygiene and sport, research and studies, multiple use, hunting, state security); 3) Ability to produce timber in the long run of at least 1 m3 of bole timber per 1 ha a year. Increment on forest land with small productivity may be lower"@en .

ex:Def_def_est_unknown_854_criteria ex:minArea "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "1.3"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "1.3"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_est_unknown_855 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest land [metsamaa] - Land which is used for forestry. Forest land can be div..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Estonia ;
    ex:country "Estonia" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Forest land [metsamaa] - Land which is used for forestry. Forest land can be divided: - land with forest (stands), - land without forest (not regenerated cut over areas, treeless, openings, burnt woodlands, perished stands) - young silvicultures which are not yet dense enough, - tree nurseries. Estonian Encyclopedia From: \"Sulev Svilponis\" Sulev@ekm.envir.ee. Definitions are unofficial translations"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_1993_106 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest (for inventory) - forest tree stands with at least 20 % canopy closure at..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_1993_106_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forest (for inventory) - forest tree stands with at least 20 % canopy closure at rotation age (closed forests according to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO) definition). However, for certain types of woodland which are characteristic of the Mediterranean region (maquis and similar woodland, stands of Quercus suber and Quercus ilex, etc.), this canopy closure should be at least 10 %. The minimum size of the forests to be sampled be 0.5 hectare"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_1993_106_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_1993_22 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "For land use, various approaches are proposed into the literature. Two main \"sch..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "For land use, various approaches are proposed into the literature. Two main \"schools\" may be distinguished. Land use in terms of functional dimension corresponds to the description of areas in terms of their socio-economic purpose: areas used for residential, industrial or commercial purposes, for farming or forestry, for recreational or conservation purposes, etc. Links with land cover are possible; it may be possible to infer land use from land cover and conversely. But situations are often complicated and the link is not so evident. Another approach, termed sequential, has been particularly developed for agricultural purposes. The definition is a series of operations on land, carried out by humans, with the intention to obtain products and/or benefits through using land resources. For example a sequence of operations such as ploughing, seeding, weeding, fertilising and harvesting (MÜCHER et al. 1993). Contrary to land cover, land use is difficult to \"observe\". For example, it is often difficult to decide if grasslands are used or not for agricultural purposes. The information coming from the source of observation may not be sufficient and may require additional information. In the case of agricultural use, farmers may bring information, for example if cattle are present or not, if they are grazing. It is also possible to use characteristics on the spot indicating the presence or absence of cattle. For the FUNCTIONAL approach, inference from land cover may be helpful. For the SEQUENTIAL approach, a more exhaustive recording of various attributes will be needed, for example a multi-temporal approach. In the following, land use will be understood as FUNCTIONAL"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2000_124 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "TBFRA 2000 requires that the crown cover is greater than 10 % and tree height is..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_2000_124_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "TBFRA 2000 requires that the crown cover is greater than 10 % and tree height is greater than 5 meters"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2000_124_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_2000_1432 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "2000. Other wooded land is land which has some forestry characteristics but is n..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "2000. Other wooded land is land which has some forestry characteristics but is not forest as defined above. It includes open woodland and scrub, shrub and brushland, whether or not used for pasture or range. It excludes land occupied by \"trees outside the forest.”"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2000_1433 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "2000. Wooded area - includes forest area and other types of wooded land. and" ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "2000. Wooded area - includes forest area and other types of wooded land. and"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2000_98 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest land is defined as land with tree crown cover (stand density) of more tha..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_2000_98_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forest land is defined as land with tree crown cover (stand density) of more than about 20% of the area. Continuous forest with trees usually growing to more than about 7 m in height and able to produce wood. This includes both closed forest formations where trees of various storeys and undergrowth cover a high proportion of the ground, and open forest formations with a continuous grass layer in which tree synusiae cover at least 10% of the ground"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2000_98_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "7.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "7.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_2004_99 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land with tree crown cover (or equivalent stocking level) of more than 10 % and ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_2004_99_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Land with tree crown cover (or equivalent stocking level) of more than 10 % and area of more than 0.5 ha. The trees should be able to reach a minimum height of 5 m at maturity in situ"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_2004_99_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
