ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_1555 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The establishment of a more or less contiguous tree cover, normally by planting,..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.14 STAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.1.2 Land Cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.1 AFFORESTATION - Afforestation may be a legal term or a process of stocking land. In the case of the latter, we group the definitions into those that imply a change in land cover only and those that imply a change in land cover and use. Four definitions specify \"crops\" implying that the trees will be later removed for commercial purposes. The time period for lands \"never having been forested\" vary from never to 30 to 100 years." ;
    skos:note "The establishment of a more or less contiguous tree cover, normally by planting, in areas which have previously been without trees"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_1663 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The restoration of tree cover, to areas from which trees have recently been remo..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.4 FORESTATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.5.1 Restoration of Land Cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "The restoration of tree cover, to areas from which trees have recently been removed, by planting, encouragement of natural regeneration, or a combination of these methods"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_173 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land with tree crown cover of more than 10 percent and area of more than 0.5 ha...." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_173_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Land with tree crown cover of more than 10 percent and area of more than 0.5 ha. The trees should be able to reach a minimum height of 5m at maturity in situ. Young natural stands and all plantations established for forestry purposes which have yet to reach a crown density of 10 percent or tree height of 5m are included under forest, as are areas normally forming part of the forest area but which are temporarily unstocked as a result of human intervention or natural causes but which are expected to revert to forest. Also included are forest nurseries and seed orchards and other small open areas within the forest, forest in national parks, nature reserves and other protected areas, windbreaks and shelterbelts of trees with an area of more than 0.5 ha and a width of more than 20m"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_173_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidth "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidthSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_180 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "forest is defined as land with tree crown cover (or equivalent stocking level) o..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_180_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "forest is defined as land with tree crown cover (or equivalent stocking level) of more than 10 per cent and area more than 0.5 ha. The trees should be able to reach a minimum height of 5 m at maturity in situ. Included are: • young natural stands and all plantations established for forestry purposes which have yet to reach the crown density of 10 percent or tree height of 5 m. • areas normally forming part of the forest area which are temporarily unstocked as a result of human intervention or natural causes but which are expected to revert to forest. • forest roads, cleared tracts, firebreaks and other small open areas, as well as forest nurseries and seed orchards that constitute an integral part of the forest. • forest in national parks, nature reserves and other protected areas such as those of special environmental, scientific, historical, cultural or spiritual interest. • windbreaks and shelter belts of trees with an area of more than 0.5 ha and a width of more than 20 m. • rubber wood plantations and cork oak stands. Excluded is the land predominantly used for agricultural practices"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_180_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidth "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidthSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_19 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land use corresponds to the socio-economic description (functional dimension) of..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "Land use corresponds to the socio-economic description (functional dimension) of areas: areas used for residential, industrial or commercial purposes, for farming or forestry, for recreational or conservation purposes, etc. Links with land cover are possible; it may be possible to infer land use from land cover and conversely. But situations are often complicated and the link is not so evident. Contrary to land cover, land use is difficult to 'observe'. For example, it is often difficult to decide if grasslands are used or not for agricultural purposes. Distinctions between land use and land cover and their definition have impacts on the development of classification systems, data collection and information systems in general. and"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_20 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The socio-economic description (functional dimension) of areas: areas used for r..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "The socio-economic description (functional dimension) of areas: areas used for residential, industrial or commercial purposes, for farming or forestry, for recreational or conservation purposes, etc. Links with land cover are possible; it may be possible to infer land use from land cover and conversely. But situations are often complicated and the link is not so evident. Contrary to land cover, land use is difficult to \"observe\". For example, it is often difficult to decide if grasslands are used or not for agricultural purposes. Distinctions between land use and land cover and their definition have impacts on the development of classification systems, data collection and information systems in general"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_21 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Corresponds to the socio-economic description (functional dimension) of areas: a..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "Corresponds to the socio-economic description (functional dimension) of areas: areas used for residential, industrial or commercial purposes, for farming or forestry, for recreational or conservation purposes, etc. Links with land cover are possible; it may be possible to infer land use from land cover and conversely. But situations are often complicated and the link is not so evident. Contrary to land cover, land use is difficult to 'observe'. For example, it is often difficult to decide if grasslands are used or not for agricultural purposes. Distinctions between land use and land cover and their definition have impacts on the development of classification systems, data collection and information systems in general"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_222 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "applied. In Scandinavia, the concept of forest is based on productivity as a res..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_222_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "applied. In Scandinavia, the concept of forest is based on productivity as a result of conventional forest management objectives. The annual growth of timber must be greater than 1m3/ha if an area is to be described as productive forest. For forest land where the mean annual increment of growing stock is typically 0,1-1m3/ha, the term scrubland is used. Forest land where the increment is less than 0.1m3/ha is called wasteland. In layman’s language, the term forest implies productive forest land only"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_222_criteria ex:minHeight "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "1.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_40 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "ETYMOLOGY: Middle English, from Old French, from Medieval Latin forestis (silva)..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Administrative" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    skos:note "ETYMOLOGY: Middle English, from Old French, from Medieval Latin forestis (silva), outside (forest), from Latin for s, outside. In several European languages, the root for(s) today still means outsider -(e.g. foreigner). The word “forest” in Medieval England came to mean land outside cultivation and belonging by default to the Crown. There is no direct reference to trees or woodland. The term \"forest\" was a legal and did not mean that the whole area was wooded. “The word foresta appears for the first time in the laws of the Longobards and the capitularies of Charlemagne, referring not to woodlands in general but only to the royal game preserves. The obscure Latin verb forestare meant 'to keep out, to place off limits, to exclude.' In effect, during the Merovingian period in which the word foresta entered the lexicon, kings had taken it upon themselves to place public bans on vast tracts of woodlands in order to insure the survival of their wildlife, which in turn would insure the survival of a fundamental royal ritual--the hunt"@en .

ex:Def_def_eur_unknown_75 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "natural woodland vegetation comprising native species forming forests of tall tr..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_European_Union ;
    ex:country "European Union" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_European_Union ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.1 General definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.1.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "natural woodland vegetation comprising native species forming forests of tall trees, with typical undergrowth, and meeting the following criteria: rare or residual, and/or hosting species of Community interest (28)"@en .

ex:Def_def_fij_1992_465 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "In the National Forest Inventory (1991-1992) on a scale of 1:50,000 the minimum ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Fiji ;
    ex:country "Fiji" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_fij_1992_465_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "In the National Forest Inventory (1991-1992) on a scale of 1:50,000 the minimum forested area shows on the map is 4 ha. There is no legal definition of what a forest is, although it is classified into three: Dense forest - Crown density by trees and/or ferns 75-100% and grown coverage of grass, palm and /or bamboo 0-25%. Medium dense - Crown density by trees and/or ferns 45-80% and ground coverage by grass, palm and/or bamboo 20-55%. Scattered forest (including scattered coconut stands), dens bushland (thickets) with single trees - crown density by trees and/or ferns 15-50% and ground coverage by grass, palm and/or bamboo 50-80%. Malakai Sevudredre, Conservator of Forests, F.9641/2. 01/06/2000"@en .

