ex:Def_def_ken_2005_889 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forests are often thought of as areas with continuous tree cover consisting eith..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Kenya ;
    ex:country "Kenya" ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Forests are often thought of as areas with continuous tree cover consisting either of naturally occurring (indigenous) tree species or plantations. However, this definition ignores the importance of open savannah or dry land forests in which individual trees are scattered over wide areas of grassland. It also fails to cover small groups of trees planted on farms and other cultivated land. It is these dry land forests and farm trees that provide important livelihood strategies for many of Kenya’s poor people, including wood fuel, charcoal burning for income, and use of wood products for building. Forest Act 2005"@en .

