    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Sont considérées comme forêts les formations végétales dont les produits princilpaux sont les bois, ou d'autres matières telles aue Ecorce, Tanin, Gomme. ARTICLE 2. LEX-FAOC002244. MAURITANIA: Ordonnance n&ordm; 82-171 portant Code forestier. Date of text: 15 December 1982"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1982_602_criteria ex:minHeight "2244.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "2244.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1983_603 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Includes a national forest and all land which is not under cultivation and which..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Includes a national forest and all land which is not under cultivation and which is covered by trees (The Forests and Reserves Act 1983"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1984_1518 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Stand density - A quantitative measure of tree stocking usually expressed in ter..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.11 STOCKING (Stand Density, Crown Cover)" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.9 SEMI-NATURAL FOREST See: Lund, H. Gyde. 2016 revised. Definitions of old growth, pristine, climax, ancient forests, degradation, desertification, forest fragmentation, and similar terms. [Online publication], Gainesville, VA: Forest Information Services. Misc. pagination. DOI: 10.13140/RG.2.1.2963.73612.10" ;
    skos:note "Stand density - A quantitative measure of tree stocking usually expressed in terms of the absolute number of trees, basal area, volume, or other criteria on a per unit area basis (Wenger 1984)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1984_544 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The term \"forest\" only appears in the Forestry Act 1984, where it is not defined..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "The term \"forest\" only appears in the Forestry Act 1984, where it is not defined. The context of its usage means a large woodland. \"IOM Gov DAFF\" <daff@gov.im"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1984_68 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest is a natural and territorial complex of a different rank (from a facies t..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.1 General definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.1.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forest is a natural and territorial complex of a different rank (from a facies to a geographical country) in which phytocoenoses are dominated by spontaneously developing tree vegetation. Ref. Kireev D.M. 1984. Ecological and Georaphical Terms in Forestry. Reference Glossary, Nauka, Novosibirsk, 1984, page 63"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1985_1505 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Woodlot - A restricted area of woodland, usually privately maintained as a sourc..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Woodlot - A restricted area of woodland, usually privately maintained as a source of fuel, posts, and lumber"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1985_65 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest is one of major types of vegetation, which comprises an aggregate of wood..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.1 General definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.1.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forest is one of major types of vegetation, which comprises an aggregate of woody, srubs, herbaceous and other (mosses, liches) plants, including fauna and microorganismus; all these components are biologically interacted during their development, and impact both each other and environment. Ref. Forest Encyclopaedia, Vol 1, Soviet Encyclopaedia, Moscow, 1985, page 503"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1985_826 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Aux termes du présent Code, on entend, d'une manière générale, par forêts ou boi..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1985_826_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_United_Nations ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Aux termes du présent Code, on entend, d'une manière générale, par forêts ou boisements: 1. Les terrains recouverts d'une formation végétale à base d'arbres ou d'arbustes, que cette formation soit naturelle ou résultant de semis ou de plantations faits de main d'homme: a. capables de produite du bois ou des produits forestiers; b. ou exerçant un effet indirect sur le climat, le régime des eaux ou le sol. 2. les terrains qui étaient recouverts de forêts ou boisements récemment coupés à blanc ou incendiés, mais qui seront susceptibles de régénération naturelle ou de reboisement artificiel. LEX-FAOC002166. BURUNDI: Loi n&ordm; 1-02 portant Code forestier. Date of text: 25 March 1985"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1985_826_criteria ex:minHeight "25.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "25.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1986_915 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Article premier. - Une forêt est une association d'arbres biologiquement interdé..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1986_915_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_United_Nations ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Article premier. - Une forêt est une association d'arbres biologiquement interdépendants au cours de leur évolution et exerçant une influence sur le milieu dans un territoire plus ou moins grand. Les produits exclusifs ou principaux des forêts sont le bois d'oeuvre, le bois de service, le bois de chauffage, les résines, la gomme, les fuits et tous autres sous-produits ligneux. Art. 2 - Les périmètres de reboisement sont les terrains plantés de mains d'homme en espèces végetales ne donnant pas de produits agricoles, ainsi que les forêts naturelles enrichies par les travaux de plantation ou de sylviculture. Art. 3 - Les défrichements sont les périmètres dans lesquels la totalité ou une partie des arbres et arbustes a été coupée par l'homme en vue de s'installer ou installer une production agricole ou industrielle. Art. 4- Les périmètres de protection sont les terrains soustraits des défrichements selon les dispositions de l'art. 13 de la présente loi et qui ont fait l'objet d'un acte de classement comme tel. Art. 5 - Le domaine forestier comprend: - Les forêts définies comme telles aux articles 1 et 51, -Les périmètres de reboisement, - Les terrains soustraits des défrichements par les dispositions de l'art. 13, - Les terrains de parcours portant une végétation arborée ou arbustive, - Les jachères anciennes de 5 ans et plus. Art. 6 - Ne font pas partie du domaine forestier: - Les terrains de culture en cours d'utilisation et les vergers, - Les jachères anciennes de moins de 5 ans, -Les terrains non boisés faisant l'objet d'un titre foncier appartenant à des personnes autres que l'Etat. - Les emprises humains: agglomérations, routes.... LEX-FAOC002238. MALI: Loi n&ordm; 86-42/AN-RM portant Code forestier. Date of text: 24 March 1986"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1986_915_criteria ex:minHeight "2238.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "2238.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1987_138 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "the forest is a plant formation in which trees predominate to the extent of modi..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "the forest is a plant formation in which trees predominate to the extent of modifying the ecology of the terrain. In order to form a specific community of this kind, the trees must occupy a sufficiently large area of land and at a minimum level of density"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1987_191 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "forest stands by referring to them as “plant formations where the principal prod..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "forest stands by referring to them as “plant formations where the principal products consist of timber” 1/, to the exclusion of any other plants that are not otherwise agricultural products 2/"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1988_1341 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Any woody vegetation. LEX-FAOC005732. SIERRA LEONE: Forestry Act, 1988. Being an..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Any woody vegetation. LEX-FAOC005732. SIERRA LEONE: Forestry Act, 1988. Being an Act to make new provisions in the Law relating to Forestry in Sierra Leone and for connected purpose"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1988_192 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(and Woodland) Land under natural or planted stands of trees, whether productive..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "(and Woodland) Land under natural or planted stands of trees, whether productive or not, and includes land from which forests have been cleared but that will be reforested in the foreseeable future (FAO 1988a: 3). and"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1988_297 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Classified Forest – a national production forest, national protection forest or ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Administrative" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.1 As a declared, legal, or administrative unit" ;
    skos:note "Classified Forest – a national production forest, national protection forest or community forest. National production forest means a national forest of which the primary management objective is production of forest produce. National protection forest means a national forest of which the primary management objective is preservation of the forest environment and protection of soil, water, flora and fauna. Community forest means an area of land which is constituted as a community forest under section 18. LEX-FAOC005732. SIERRA LEONE: Forestry Act, 1988. Being an Act to make new provisions in the Law relating to Forestry in Sierra Leone and for connected purpose"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1988_743 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "On entend par forêt, toute formation végétale d'origine naturelle ou artificiell..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "On entend par forêt, toute formation végétale d'origine naturelle ou artificielle composée d'une ou plusieurs espèces forestières d'arbes, d'arbustes ou de broussailles à l'état put ou en mélange.LEX-FAOC002805. TUNISIA: Loi n&ordm; 20 portant Code Forestier. Date of text: 13 April 1988"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1989_11 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "That which overlays or currently covers the ground, especially vegetation, perma..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_USDA ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "That which overlays or currently covers the ground, especially vegetation, permanent snow and ice fields, water bodies, or structures. Barren land is also considered a ‘land cover’ although technically it is lack of cover. The term land cover can be thought of as applying to the setting in which action (one or more different land uses) takes place (USDA Forest Service 1989)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1990_118 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "‘Forests’ have a closed canopy beneath which grasses do not grow. They are disti..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "‘Forests’ have a closed canopy beneath which grasses do not grow. They are distinct from open-canopied woodlands that grow over a grass sward"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1990_209 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(Developed Countries) Land with tree crown cover of more than 20 % of the area. ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1990_209_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "(Developed Countries) Land with tree crown cover of more than 20 % of the area. Trees should be able to grow to a height of 7 m. Included are: 1. The area with young trees or coppice able to form forest in the future. 2. The area temporarily unstocked due to regeneration or damage. Minimum area outside continuous forest: 0.5 ha. Minimum width outside continuous forest: 15 m. The forest roads and firebreaks etc. narrower than minimum width of nurseries and storage less than 0.5 ha are included in the forest area. (minimum size stand; for instance 15 m x 333 m). (Koehl and Paivinen 1996)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1990_209_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "7.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "7.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidth "15.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidthSI "15.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1990_233 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Selon la Loi 90.003 du 9 Juin 1990 portant Code Forestier Centrafricain, le doma..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Administrative" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:region "Africa" ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.1 As a declared, legal, or administrative unit" ;
    skos:note "Selon la Loi 90.003 du 9 Juin 1990 portant Code Forestier Centrafricain, le domaine forestier comprend: le domaine forestier de l'Etat et le domaine forestier des collectivités et des particuliers. Le domaine forestier de l'Etat comprend: les réserves naturelles intégrales; les parcs nationaux; les réserves de faune; les forêts récréatives; les périmètres de reboisement; et les forêts de production"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1991_1018 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forestry land includes: 1. Land of forest cover, 2. Land of no forest cover is p..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Forestry land includes: 1. Land of forest cover, 2. Land of no forest cover is planned for afforestation, hereinafter called afforestation land. ORDER NO. 58-LCT/HDNN8 DATED 19-8-1991"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1991_221 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "forest is an ecosystem that offers goods, including trees that provide lumber, f..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "forest is an ecosystem that offers goods, including trees that provide lumber, fuel, and fruit. The forest may also provide services in the form of water storage and flood control, wildlife habitat, nutrient storage, and recreation"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1991_66 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "An ecological system composed of interacting biotic and abiotic components of th..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1991_66_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.1 General definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.1.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "An ecological system composed of interacting biotic and abiotic components of the environment in which trees are a major constituent, such that their canopies cover 20% or more of the area (Maini 1991)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1991_66_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1991_89 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land under natural or cultivated stands of trees intended for forest use, includ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.1 General definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.1.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Land under natural or cultivated stands of trees intended for forest use, including land cleared of trees but which will be reforested. Permanent meadows and pastures with scattered trees and shrubs do not constitute forest (Miani 1991 - Forestry Chronicle 70(6):669 1994)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_1077 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest land includes the public forest, the permanent forest or forest reserves,..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Administrative" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.1 As a declared, legal, or administrative unit" ;
    skos:note "Forest land includes the public forest, the permanent forest or forest reserves, and forest reservations. Public forest is the mass of lands of the public domain which has not been subject to the present system of classification for the determination of which lands are needed for forest purposes and which are not. Permanent forest or forest reserves refers to those lands of the public domain which have been the subject of the present system of classification and declared as not needed for forest purposes"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_193 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land under natural or planted stand of trees, whether productive or not. Include..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Land under natural or planted stand of trees, whether productive or not. Included are shrub land, savannah etc., land from which forests have been cleared but that will be reforested in the foreseeable future, area occupied by forest roads, small cleared tracts and other small open areas within the forest and which constitute an integral part of the forest. [Sources: FAO yearbook Production. 1992. Vol. 46. FAO Statistics Series No. 112. 281 pp"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_428 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "An area of at least 1 ha covered with forest tree vegetation and crown cover mor..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1992_428_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_United_Nations ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "An area of at least 1 ha covered with forest tree vegetation and crown cover more than 10 percent. A.K. Christou \"Forestry Department - Research and Publicity Sector\" publicity@cytanet.com.cy. Cyprus (open woodland) Forest area with only few trees per ha (that is crown cover about 1-2 percent) (UN/ECE 1992 p. 125)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_428_criteria ex:minArea "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_470 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land bearing stands of the main forest-forming species as well as forest plantat..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_United_Nations ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Land bearing stands of the main forest-forming species as well as forest plantations, nurseries, and the like. Forests do not include roads or other land not subject to afforestation. …Other wooded land includes scrub and plantation in which trees are rare. This category also conventionally includes clearings, burnt areas, and glades subject to stocking in the future…. (UN/ECE 1992 p. 275)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_570 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forests – areas suitable for forest production and having a tree cover with a cr..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1992_570_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forests – areas suitable for forest production and having a tree cover with a crown density of at least 20%, and supporting trees with a dbh more than 10cm, covering at least 0.5 ha (LSFP 92)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_570_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_90 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "A classification of land use in the Natural Resources Inventory (NRI). It includ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1992_90_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.1 General definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.1.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "A classification of land use in the Natural Resources Inventory (NRI). It includes areas where trees cover at least 10% of the land and must be at least an acre in size. Forestland was found on 395 million acres, almost 30% of all private lands, in the 1992 NRI. and"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1992_90_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "395.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "395.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1993_135 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous stand of trees at least 10 m tall, their crowns interlocking; - Rain ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1993_135_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_United_Nations ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Continuous stand of trees at least 10 m tall, their crowns interlocking; - Rain forest, without any significant dry season and with trees usually more than 30 m high; - Dry forest, with dry season of several weeks or months; - Semi-evergreen forest, where some species are deciduous but understory mostly evergreen Deciduous forest, where trees lose their leaves simultaneously for months - Undifferentiated forest, with very heterogeneous patterns"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1993_135_criteria ex:minHeight "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "10.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1993_139 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forests are ecological systems with minimum crown coverage of land surface (here..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1993_139_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forests are ecological systems with minimum crown coverage of land surface (here assumed as 10 percent) and generally associated with wild flora, fauna and natural soil conditions; and not subject to agronomic practices. For the present assessments, a tree is defined as a woody perennial with a single main stem (except in coppice crops where multiple stems replace a single stem), a more or less definite crown and a minimum height of more than 5 meters on maturity. Only forest areas more than 100 ha (minimum area) are considered. and"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1993_139_criteria ex:minArea "100.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "100.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1993_1458 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Woodland. An open stand of trees at least 8 m tall with a canopy cover of 40% or..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1993_1458_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_United_Nations ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Woodland. An open stand of trees at least 8 m tall with a canopy cover of 40% or more. The field layer is usually dominated by grasses"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1993_1458_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "40.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "40.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "8.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "8.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1993_1487 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "A woodland is a stand of trees up to 18m high with a canopy cover of 20-80%. Gro..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1993_1487_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "A woodland is a stand of trees up to 18m high with a canopy cover of 20-80%. Ground layer is dominated by grasses (Timberlake, Nobanda & Mapaure, 1993; Pratt, Greenway & Gwynne, 1966). (Isaac Mapaure. Researchgate. 23 Jan 2014).g"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1993_1487_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "80.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "80.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "18.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "18.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1994_1314 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Trees. are defined as ..any woody plant regardless of its, species, age, size in..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1994_1314_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UNFCCC ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Trees. are defined as ..any woody plant regardless of its, species, age, size including bamboo, reeds and palms as well as other plants to be designated as .trees by the Ministry. (Art.2.4). The definition given to .forest. and .trees. in the SNNPRS Forestry Proclamation is similar to that given in the Federal Proclamation (Arts.2.3 and 2.8 of Proclamation No.77/2004)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1994_1314_criteria ex:minHeight "2.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "2.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1994_39 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The purpose to which land is put by humans (e.g., protected areas, forestry for ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "The purpose to which land is put by humans (e.g., protected areas, forestry for timber products, plantations, row-crop agriculture, pastures, or human settlements) (Turner and Meyer 1994). Change in land use may or may not cause a significant change in land cover. For example, change from selectively harvested forest to protected forest will not cause much discernible cover change in the short term, but change to cultivated land will cause a large change in cover"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1994_458 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The Forestry Conservation, Development and Utilization Proclamation. was issued ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1994_458_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UNFCCC ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "The Forestry Conservation, Development and Utilization Proclamation. was issued at the Federal level in 1994 (Proclamation No.94/1994). The term.forest. is defined as ..a community of plants either naturally grown or developed by planting and in many respects are trees and other plants having a woody character. (Art.2.3). Forests are thus defined into natural forests (e.g. naturally growing indigenous trees) and forests established by planting (e.g. afforestation or reforestation). The definition given to .forest. and .trees. in the SNNPRS Forestry Proclamation is similar to that given in the Federal Proclamation (Arts.2.3 and 2.8 of Proclamation No.77/2004)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1994_458_criteria ex:minHeight "2.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "2.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1994_720 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest – a vegetation type dominated by trees, which may be pristine rainforest,..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forest – a vegetation type dominated by trees, which may be pristine rainforest, scrub woodland; Palm savanna or plantation (Wong 2000). According to FAO (1994) Forests are ecosystems with a minimlum of 10% cover of trees or bamboos"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_140 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Ecosystems with a minimum of 10% cover of trees and/or bamboos, generally associ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Ecosystems with a minimum of 10% cover of trees and/or bamboos, generally associated with wild flora, fauna and natural soil conditions, and not subject to agricultural practices. The term forest is further subdivided, according to its origin, into two categories: (i) Natural forests: a subset of forest composed of tree species known to be indigenous to the area; and; (ii) Plantation forests: established artificially by afforestation on lands which previously did not carry forests within living memory; establishment artificially by reforestation of land which carried forest before, and involving the replacement of the indigenous species by a new and essentially different species or genetic variety. Definition applies for developing countries"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_1401 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Woody plant that renews its growth every year (called a perennial). Most plants ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Woody plant that renews its growth every year (called a perennial). Most plants classified as trees have a single self-supporting trunk containing woody tissue and in most species the trunk produces secondary limbs, called branches. Encyclopedia Britannia 1995"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_141 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land with tree crown cover (stand density) of more than about 20 percent of the ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1995_141_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Land with tree crown cover (stand density) of more than about 20 percent of the area. Continuous forest with trees usually growing to more than about 7 m in height and able to produce wood. This includes both closed forest formations where trees of various storeys and undergrowth cover a high proportion of the ground, and open forest formations with a continuous grass layer in which tree synusia cover at least 10 percent of the ground. Definition applies for developed countries"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_141_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "7.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "7.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_194 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(and woodland) - Includes woodlot or timber tracts, natural or planted, constitu..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "(and woodland) - Includes woodlot or timber tracts, natural or planted, constituting part of the holding which have or will have value as wood, timber, or other forest products or for protection. Forest tree nurseries should be included in this category. Rows, belts and small clumps of trees, bamboo and other woody vegetation should be included.... Proposed definition for the World Census for Agriculture 2000. (FAO 1995)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_195 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "land under natural or planted stands of trees, whether productive or not, includ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1995_195_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "land under natural or planted stands of trees, whether productive or not, including land from which forest has been cleared but that will be reforested in the foreseeable future, and including areas occupied by roads, small cleared tracts and other small open areas within the forest which constitute an integral part of the forest"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_195_criteria ex:minHeight "34883.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "34883.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_228 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Any land and water body, whether or not under vegetation cover, in which no pers..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Administrative" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.1 As a declared, legal, or administrative unit" ;
    skos:note "Any land and water body, whether or not under vegetation cover, in which no person has acquired a permanent and transferable right of use and occupancy, whether such land is located inside or outside the forest boundary pillars, and includes land registered in a person's name as Tsamgrog (grazing land) or Sokshing (woodlot for collection of leaf litter). LEX-FAOC007101. BHUTAN: Forest and Nature Conservation Act of Bhutan, 1995. An Act to provide for the protection and sustainable use of forests, wildlife and related natural resources of Bhutan for the benefit of present and future generations. Also 2004"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1995_9 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land cover is the biophysical state of the earth’s surface and immediate subsurf..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "Land cover is the biophysical state of the earth’s surface and immediate subsurface\" (Turner et al. 1995, 20). In other words, land cover \"describes the physical state of the land surface: as in cropland, mountains, or forests\" (Meyer 1995, 25 cited in Moser 1996, 247). Meyer and Turner (1994) add: \"it embraces, for example, the quantity and type of surface vegetation, water, and earth materials (Meyer and Turner 1994, 5). Moser (1996) notes that: \"The term originally referred to the type of vegetation that covered the land surface, but has broadened subsequently to include human structures, such as buildings or pavement, and other aspects of the physical environment, such as soils, biodiversity, and surfaces and groundwater\" (Moser 1996, 247)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_1390 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest tree, a tree of the forest, especially a timber tree, as distinguished fr..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1996_1390_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Forest tree, a tree of the forest, especially a timber tree, as distinguished from a fruit tree. Webster's Revised Unabridged Dictionary, © 1996, 1998 MICRA, Inc"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_1390_criteria ex:minHeight "1998.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "1998.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_1441 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Area with tree crown cover of 5-20% of the surface. Trees should be able to grow..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1996_1441_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Area with tree crown cover of 5-20% of the surface. Trees should be able to grow to a height of more than 7 m (Koehl and Paivinen 1996) Woodlands, almost exclusively of Aleppo Pine, with at least 20% ground cover by trees"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_1441_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "7.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "7.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_181 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forests - whether natural, modified or planted - are biotic communities characte..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_United_Nations ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Forests - whether natural, modified or planted - are biotic communities characterized by a predominance of woody vegetation. They are found in all climatic zones (boreal, temperate, tropical and mountainous) and may take the form of closed canopy forests or open canopy woodlands. They comprise all the components of forest ecosystems and their natural resources. Forest lands may be covered by forests or degraded - that is, currently devoid of trees or not maintained in a sustainable manner (UNITED NATIONS 1996)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_205 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land under natural or planted stands of trees with tree crown cover (or equivale..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1996_205_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Land under natural or planted stands of trees with tree crown cover (or equivalent stocking level) of more than 10 percent and area of more than 0.5 ha. whose primary use is forestry. The trees should be able to reach a minimum height of 5 m at maturity in situ. Includes: closed forest formations where trees of various storeys and undergrowth cover a height portion of the ground and open forest formations with a continuous vegetation layer in with tree crown cover exceeds 10 percent; plantations primarily used for forestry purposes, including rubberwood plantations and cork oak stands; areas normally forming part of the forest land area which are temporarily unstocked as a result of human intervention or natural causes but which are expected to revert to stocked forest; young natural stands and all plantations established for forestry purposes which have yet to reach a crown density of 10 percent; forest roads, cleared tracts, such as firebreaks and other small open areas, as well as forest in national parks, nature reserves and other protected areas such as those of specific environmental, scientific, historical, cultural or spiritual interest; windbreak and shelterbelts of an area of more than 0.5 ha and width of more than 20 m. (Anon. 1996)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_205_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidth "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidthSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_72 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land with tree crown cover of more than 20 % of the area. Trees should be able t..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1996_72_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.1 General definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.1.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Land with tree crown cover of more than 20 % of the area. Trees should be able to grow to a height of 7 m. Minimum area outside continuous forest: 0.5 ha. Minimum width outside continuous forest: 15 m. (minimum size stand; for instance 15 m x 333 m). (Köhl and Päivinen 1996)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1996_72_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "7.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "7.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidth "15.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidthSI "15.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_1046 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest is the aggregate of forest vegetation, land, fauna and other components o..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Forest is the aggregate of forest vegetation, land, fauna and other components of the natural environment that are of great ecological, economic and social importance. Ref. Forest Code of the Russian Federation, adopted by the State Duma of the Russian Federation 22 January 1997, page 3, Moscow, 1997"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_114 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "An ecosystem in which the dominant plants are trees; woodlands are distinguished..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_IPCC ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "An ecosystem in which the dominant plants are trees; woodlands are distinguished from forests by their lower density of trees"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_12 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The observed (bio) physical cover on the earth's surface. Also aspects describin..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "The observed (bio) physical cover on the earth's surface. Also aspects describing land itself rather than land cover have been included (e.g. bare areas, waterbodies, etc.) because in practice the scientific community is used to describe those aspects under the term land cover (Di Gregorio and Jansen 1997, 1998, Jansen and Di Gregorio 1998, Choudhury and Jansen 1998.)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_122 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "All wooded areas with greater than +/- 70% tree canopy cover, where the dominant..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1997_122_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "All wooded areas with greater than +/- 70% tree canopy cover, where the dominant canopy is greater than +/- 5m in thight (minimum height). Essentially indigenous tree species growing under natural or semi-natural conditions (although it may include some localised areas of self-seeded exotic species). Excludes planted forests (and woodlots). (Trevett 1997)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_122_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "70.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "70.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_128 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Land with tree crown cover (or equivalent stocking level) of more than 10 percen..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1997_128_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Land with tree crown cover (or equivalent stocking level) of more than 10 percent and area of more than 0.5 ha. The trees should be able to reach a minimum height of 5 m at maturity in situ. May consist either of closed forest formations where trees of various storeys and undergrowth cover a high portion of the ground; or of open forest formations where trees of various storeys and undergrowth cover a high portion of the ground; or of open forest formations with a continuous vegetation cover in which tree cover exceeds 10 percent. Young natural stands and all plantations established for forestry purposes which have yet to reach a crown density of 10 percent or tree height of 5 m are included under forest, as are temporarily unstocked as a result of human intervention or natural causes but which are expected to revert to forest. Includes: Forest nurseries and seed orchards that constitute an integral part of the forest; forest roads, cleared tracts, firebreaks, and other small open areas within the forest; forest in national parks, nature reserves and other protected areas such as those of special environmental, scientific, historical, cultural, or spiritual interest; wind; windbreaks and shelterbelts of trees with an area of more than 0.5 ha and a width of more than 20 m. Rubberwood plantations and cork oak stands are included. Excludes: Land predominantly used for agricultural practices"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_128_criteria ex:minArea "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidth "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidthSI "20.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_142 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(developed countries) Land with tree crown cover (stand density) of more than ab..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1997_142_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "(developed countries) Land with tree crown cover (stand density) of more than about 20 per cent of the area. Continuous forest with trees usually growing to more than about 7 m in height and able to produce wood. This includes both closed forest formations where trees of various storeys and undergrowth cover a high proportion of the ground, and open forest formations with a continuous grass layer in which tree synusia cover at least 10 per cent of the ground (FAO 1997)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_142_criteria ex:minHeight "7.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "7.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_1561 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest stands established artificially on lands that previously have not support..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_IPCC ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.14 STAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.1.2 Land Cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.1 AFFORESTATION - Afforestation may be a legal term or a process of stocking land. In the case of the latter, we group the definitions into those that imply a change in land cover only and those that imply a change in land cover and use. Four definitions specify \"crops\" implying that the trees will be later removed for commercial purposes. The time period for lands \"never having been forested\" vary from never to 30 to 100 years." ;
    skos:note "Forest stands established artificially on lands that previously have not supported forests for more than 50 years"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_1590 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The establishment of a tree crop on an area from which it has always or very lon..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.14 STAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.1.3 Land Cover and Use" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.1 AFFORESTATION - Afforestation may be a legal term or a process of stocking land. In the case of the latter, we group the definitions into those that imply a change in land cover only and those that imply a change in land cover and use. Four definitions specify \"crops\" implying that the trees will be later removed for commercial purposes. The time period for lands \"never having been forested\" vary from never to 30 to 100 years." ;
    skos:note "The establishment of a tree crop on an area from which it has always or very long been absent. Where such establishment fails and is repeated, the latter may properly be termed reafforestation (FAO 1997)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_1667 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest stands established artificially on lands that have supported forests with..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_IPCC ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.4 FORESTATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.5.1 Restoration of Land Cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "Forest stands established artificially on lands that have supported forests within the last 50 years"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_1682 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Establishment of a tree crop on forest land (FAO 1997)" ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.4 FORESTATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.5.1 Restoration of Land Cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "Establishment of a tree crop on forest land (FAO 1997)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_1706 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Areas that become forested through the ordinary process of forest succession (e...." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.6 REGENERATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.6.1 By natural methods only" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "Areas that become forested through the ordinary process of forest succession (e.g. occupation of abandoned farm lands or lands laid bare by recent glaciation). Also where forest is being regenerated to the same or similar species as removed from the site. Brinkley 1997"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_196 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "(and Woodland). Land under natural or planted stands of trees, whether productiv..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "International" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.2 International definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.2.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "(and Woodland). Land under natural or planted stands of trees, whether productive or not. This category included land from which trees have been cleared but will be reforested in the foreseeable future, but it excludes woodland or forest used only for recreation purposes. Stands of permanent crops such as rubber, fruit trees, nut trees, are classed as permanent crops under agricultural lands. [Source: FAO Production Yearbook. 10.10.97. and"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_36 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The arrangements, activities and inputs people undertake in a certain land cover..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.1 LAND COVER" ;
    skos:note "The arrangements, activities and inputs people undertake in a certain land cover type to produce, change or maintain it (Di Gregorio and Jansen 1997, 1998, Jansen and Di Gregorio 1998). A given land use may take place on one, or more than one, piece of land and several land uses may occur on the same piece of land. Definition of land use in this way provides a basis for precise and quantitative economic and environmental impact analysis and permits precise distinctions between land uses, if required. Lands may be devoid of forest cover, but may be used for forestry purposes. Similarly, lands may have tree cover, but not used for forestry use – i.e. \"urban forests.\""@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_584 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Focuses on growing stock and applies to measurable criteria - width of forest ar..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1997_584_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Focuses on growing stock and applies to measurable criteria - width of forest area, crown closure and top height. Width of growing stock with canopy closure = 100% is at least 25 m and with crown closure = 20% at least 50 m. Canopy closure between 20 % and 100 % depending on width of growing stock. Minimum top diameter 3 m (exceptions afforestation, young growth, mountain alder, mountain pine) (European Communities 1997b - p. 1067)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_584_criteria ex:minHeight "25.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "25.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidth "25.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minWidthSI "25.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_627 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest = areas with tree height 5 m, Savanna/shrub = areas with tree height <5 m..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1997_627_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forest = areas with tree height 5 m, Savanna/shrub = areas with tree height <5 m. Canopy cover 20%. Moses Chakanga. chakanga@forestry.met.gov.na and (Trevett 1997)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_627_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "20.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "5.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "5.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_825 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Au sens du present Code, sont consideres comme forets les espaces occupes par de..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_au_BF_d_apres_le_Code_Forestier_au_Burkina_Faso_ministere_de_l_environnement_et_ ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Au sens du present Code, sont consideres comme forets les espaces occupes par des formations vegetales d'arbres et d'arbustes, a l'exclusion de celles resultant d'activites agricoles (all area but town and agricultural lands) Source: au BF d'apres le Code Forestier au Burkina Faso (ministere de l'environnement et de l'eau, 1997) Loi n[006/97/ADP du 31 janvier 1997, portant Code Forestier au Burkina FAso, Article 12. Via Daniel-Yyves Alexandre"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1997_882 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Coillte estate - High forests- commercial forests and plantable bare land - capa..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Use" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.3 As a land use type" ;
    skos:note "Coillte estate - High forests- commercial forests and plantable bare land - capable of growing trees. (European Communities 1997a - p. 401)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1327 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Tree - Include any seedlings, saplings, transplant or coopice shoot of any age. ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Tree - Include any seedlings, saplings, transplant or coopice shoot of any age. Forestry Act 1998. Via David May ISTF CVP Lesotho"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1339 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "A woody perennial plant, typically large and with a well-defined stem or stems c..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1339_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "A woody perennial plant, typically large and with a well-defined stem or stems carrying a more or less definite crown --note sometimes defined as attaining a minimum diameter of 5 in (12.7 cm) and a minimum height of 15 ft (4.6 m) at maturity, with no branches within 3 ft (1 m) of the ground\". SAF (1998)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1339_criteria ex:minHeight "4.6"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "4.6"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1352 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "A woody perennial plant with a single, well defined stem carrying a more-or-less..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1352_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "A woody perennial plant with a single, well defined stem carrying a more-or-less-defined crown and being at least 3 m tall. Note: where a clear separation between trees and shrubs is too difficult to establish, a sub-condition of Height is applied: a woody plant higher than 5 m is classified as a Tree; a woody plant lower than 5 m is classified as a shrub. This general rule is subject to the following exception: a woody plant with the clear physiognomic aspect of tree can be classified as a Tree even if the height is less than 5 m, but more than 3 m (Di Gregorio and Jansen 1998)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1352_criteria ex:minHeight "3.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "3.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1392 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Perennial plant with woody stem - usually a single stem or trunk made up of wood..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1392_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "Perennial plant with woody stem - usually a single stem or trunk made up of wood and protected by an outer layer of bark. It absorbs water through a root system. There is no clear dividing line between shrubs and trees, but sometimes minimum height of 6 m (20 ft.) is used to define a tree. Lindley and Moore 1998"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1392_criteria ex:minHeight "6.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "6.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1515 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Degree of a denseness of growing of trees in a tree stand describing a share of ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1515_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.11 STOCKING (Stand Density, Crown Cover)" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.9 SEMI-NATURAL FOREST See: Lund, H. Gyde. 2016 revised. Definitions of old growth, pristine, climax, ancient forests, degradation, desertification, forest fragmentation, and similar terms. [Online publication], Gainesville, VA: Forest Information Services. Misc. pagination. DOI: 10.13140/RG.2.1.2963.73612.10" ;
    skos:note "Degree of a denseness of growing of trees in a tree stand describing a share of use by them of taken space. Usually under definition of stocking (polnota) understands a relative polnota of a tree stand - ratio of the sum of areas of cross-cuts of all trees of a tree stand at height of 1.3 meters (breast-height) on 1 ha to the sum of areas of cross-cuts of a normal tree stand (with a polnota 1,0), i.e. tree stand with optimum for the given species and types of forest growing conditions the sum of areas of cross-cuts, which undertakes from the tables of growth. . [From: \" Russian Forest Encyclopedia\" \"Igor Yakovlev\" yakovlev@mari-el.ru Date: Sat, 6 Jun 1998 21:16:59 +0400]"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1515_criteria ex:minArea "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "1.3"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "1.3"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1529 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "The sum of areas of cross-cuts of all trees of a tree stand at height of 1.3 met..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1529_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.11 STOCKING (Stand Density, Crown Cover)" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.9 SEMI-NATURAL FOREST See: Lund, H. Gyde. 2016 revised. Definitions of old growth, pristine, climax, ancient forests, degradation, desertification, forest fragmentation, and similar terms. [Online publication], Gainesville, VA: Forest Information Services. Misc. pagination. DOI: 10.13140/RG.2.1.2963.73612.10" ;
    skos:note "The sum of areas of cross-cuts of all trees of a tree stand at height of 1.3 meters (breast-height) on 1 ha' is basal area. [From: \"Maksym Polyakov\" <mpoliak@pcomp.usau.kiev.ua Date: Wed, 17 Jun 1998 11:20:37 +0300 (MSD)]"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1529_criteria ex:minArea "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minAreaSI "1.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "1.3"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "1.3"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1615 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Refers to change of land cover with depletion of tree crown cover to less than 1..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1615_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.2 DEFORESTATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.2.2 As a change in land cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.1 AFFORESTATION - Afforestation may be a legal term or a process of stocking land. In the case of the latter, we group the definitions into those that imply a change in land cover only and those that imply a change in land cover and use. Four definitions specify \"crops\" implying that the trees will be later removed for commercial purposes. The time period for lands \"never having been forested\" vary from never to 30 to 100 years." ;
    skos:note "Refers to change of land cover with depletion of tree crown cover to less than 10%. Changes within the forest class (e.g. from closed to open forest) which negatively affect the stand or site and, in particular, lower the production capacity, are termed forest degradation"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1615_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1678 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Process of establishing of forest cultures on before forested areas. (Forest cul..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.4 FORESTATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.5.1 Restoration of Land Cover" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "Process of establishing of forest cultures on before forested areas. (Forest cultures (specific Russian term) - forest, which was created by seeding or planting). [From: \"Igor Yakovlev\" yakovlev@mari-el.ru Date: Wed, 10 Jun 1998 02:51:19 -0400 (EDT)]"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_1707 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Regrowth of forest vegetation, in the absence of planting or direct seeding, fol..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "3.6 REGENERATION" ;
    ex:sectionSub "3.6.1 By natural methods only" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "Regrowth of forest vegetation, in the absence of planting or direct seeding, following any of many kinds of disturbances that were lethal to forest vegetation (e.g. landslide, fire, pest damage, wind, logging, clearing for agriculture) previously growing on the site. Stanley 1998"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_1998_275 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest reserve - any area which has been declared as such by the King or (now) t..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
