ex:Def_def_unk_2000_1459 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "‘‘Wodland’’ refers to the type of land cover characterized by trees and shrubs: ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_2000_1459_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_UN-FAO ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.4 TREE" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.5 WOODS, WOODLAND, OTHER WOODED LANDS (OWL)." ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "‘‘Wodland’’ refers to the type of land cover characterized by trees and shrubs: ‘‘other wooded land.’’ Other wooded land, or OWL, is defined by FRA-2000 as land with a tree crown cover (or equivalent stocking level) of 5–10% of trees able to reach a height of 5 meters at maturity, a crown cover of more than 10% of trees not able to reach a height of 5 meters at maturity (such as dwarf or stunted trees), or shrub and bush cover of more than 10%. OWL excludes areas with the tree, shrub, or bush cover just specified but of less than 0.5 hectares and width of 20 meters, as well as land predominantly used for agricultural practices (FAO 2000, 2001c)"@en .

