    ex:sectionSub "2.4.1 Definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.4.4 Ecological/Miscellaneous Definitions" ;
    skos:note "“Tree” means forest trees including bushes, palms, bamboos, shrubs, brushwood, creepers and climbers at all stages of their growth; (GOSS, 2009)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_2009_1742 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Global Observation of Forest and Land Cover Dynamics(GOFC-GOLD). 2009. A source ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Ecological/Miscellaneous" ;
    ex:geographicScope "Local/State" ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "5.5 Considerations" ;
    ex:sectionSub "5.3.2 From a Land Cover Interpretation - If taken literally" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "3.5 REFORESTATION - Reforestation is sorted by restoration of land cover and land cover and use. Several specified the planting of \"crops.\"" ;
    skos:note "Global Observation of Forest and Land Cover Dynamics(GOFC-GOLD). 2009. A source book of methods and Procedures for monitoring and reporting and anthropogenic greenhouse gas emissions and removals caused by deforestation, gains and losses of carbon stocks in forest remaining forest, and forestation"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_2009_397 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest and woodland defined as a class of vegetation characterized by a closed t..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_2009_397_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forest and woodland defined as a class of vegetation characterized by a closed tree canopy, with interlocking crowns generally providing 60-100% cover. “Woodland”, by comparison, is characterized by an open canopy, with tree crowns constituting just 25-60% cover. The canopy height of forest and woodland ranges from about 16m, down to about 4.5m in height, below which shrubland species dominate. Burton (2008b)"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_2009_397_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "60.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "60.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeight "16.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minHeightSI "16.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_2009_504 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "Forest means an ecosystem dominated by woody plants, consisting of closed forest..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
    ex:definitionType "Land Cover" ;
    ex:geographicScope "National" ;
    ex:hasCriteria ex:Def_def_unk_2009_504_criteria ;
    ex:hasSource ex:Source_Unknown ;
    ex:sectionMain "2.3 FOREST/FOREST LAND" ;
    ex:sectionSub "2.3.3 National definitions" ;
    ex:sectionSubSub "2.3.3.2 As a land cover type" ;
    skos:note "Forest means an ecosystem dominated by woody plants, consisting of closed forest formations, where trees of various stories sand undergrowth cover a high proportion of the ground; or open forest with continuous vegetation cover in which tree crown cover exceeds 10 percent and includes mangrove forest and any wetlands or open lands within a forest which form an integral part of the ecosystem; forest produce in the ecosystem; and biological, soil and water resources of the ecosystem"@en .

ex:Def_def_unk_2009_504_criteria ex:minCanopyCover "10.0"^^xsd:float ;
    ex:minCanopyCoverSI "10.0"^^xsd:float .

ex:Def_def_unk_2009_688 a ex:Definition ;
    rdfs:label "National definition: Forest is land spanning more than 0.3 ha with trees taller ..." ;
    ex:appliesTo ex:Country_Unknown ;
